==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*gʷerH-}}
From {{bor|en|la|grātuītus||free}}, from {{der|en|la|grātia||favor}}, {{m|la|grātus||showing favor}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɡɹəˈtjuː.ɪt.əs/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɡɹəˈtu.ɪt.əs/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-gratuitous.ogg|a=US}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Given freely; [[unearned]].
# [[unjustified|Unjustified]] or [[unnecessary]]; not [[call for|called for]] by the circumstances.
#: {{syn|en|uncalled-for}}
#: {{co|en|'''gratuitous''' violence}}
#* {{quote-av|en|year=1983|writer=Lorenzo Semple Jr.|title={{w|Never Say Never Again}}|role={{w|Q (James Bond)|Q}}|actor=Alec McCowen|passage=Good to see you Mr. Bond. Things've been awfully dull 'round here. {{...|Bureaucrats running the whole place. Everything done by the book. Can't make a decision unless the computer gives you the go ahead.}} Now you're on this. I hope we're going to have some '''gratuitous''' sex and violence!}}
# (''[[in]] [[linguistic]] [[criticism]]'') [[extraneous|Extraneous]], [[interpolated]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019|author=[[wikipedia:Andy Griffiths (author)|Andy Griffiths]], illustrated by {{w|Terry Denton}}|title=The 117-Story Treehouse, American Edition|page=142-143|passage={{quote-gloss|Chief of the Story Police:}} “{{...}} You are hereby charged with crimes against good and proper storytelling, including the use of outlandish plots, ridiculous characters, silly names, needless repetition—I repeat, ''needless repetition''—too much detail in some places and not enough in others, lack of worthy life lessons, poor dietary habits, unlikely escapes, '''gratuitous''' violence, time-wasting chases, and worst of all, clichéd endings, such as ''it was all just a dream''...Need I go on?”}}

